 "literal": "No one else was there; he himself, the thief. / If he denies that, what shall I do? / Like a millet stem, with small green legs, / watching the aaral fish in the flowing water, / only a heron was there at the time he united with me.",
 "translation": "There was no one else there, only he, the thief. If he denies it now, what can I do? At the very time he united with me, there was only a heron with small green legs like millet stems, watching for aaral fish in the flowing water.",
 "modern": "அங்கு வேறு யாரும் இல்லை; அவன் மட்டுமே கள்வன். அவன் தன் வாக்கை மறுத்தால் நான் என்ன செய்வேன்? தினைத் தட்டை போன்ற சிறிய பசிய கால்களை உடைய, ஓடுகின்ற நீரில் ஆரல் மீனை நோக்குகின்ற நாரை மட்டுமே அவன் என்னை மணந்த பொழுது அங்கு இருந்தது.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her confidante in the kuṟiñci landscape. The hero has made promises of marriage during their secret meetings but has now delayed, and she fears he might deny them. She laments that there were no human witnesses to their union, only the hero and a heron. The heron, however, was not paying attention to them; it was entirely focused on catching the aaral fish in the running water. This image of the heron waiting for its prey reflects the hero's focused intent on his own pleasure while being oblivious to her future suffering, and highlights her helplessness as she has no witness to prove his promise.",
